WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.277
Suppose a motor should hold one thousand
revolutions per minute, but the

2
00:00:04.277 --> 00:00:07.902
sensor reports nine hundred. The controller
does not command a correction from

3
00:00:07.902 --> 00:00:08.738
the target alone.

4
00:00:08.738 --> 00:00:13.495
It first computes tracking error: the desired
output minus the measured output.

5
00:00:13.495 --> 00:00:17.339
Here, the error is positive one hundred
revolutions per minute. The

6
00:00:17.339 --> 00:00:19.705
proportional term reacts to the error right
now.

7
00:00:19.705 --> 00:00:25.052
The integral term accumulates error over time,
helping remove a persistent offset.

8
00:00:25.052 --> 00:00:29.750
The derivative term responds to how quickly
error is changing, which can

9
00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:31.557
anticipate motion and reduce overshoot.

10
00:00:31.557 --> 00:00:35.469
Their weighted sum becomes the control input
sent to the system.

11
00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:39.640
After the motor responds, the sensor measures
again and the loop repeats.

12
00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:45.066
Larger gains are not automatically better:
poor tuning can create overshoot,

13
00:00:45.066 --> 00:00:46.874
noise sensitivity, or oscillation.

14
00:00:46.874 --> 00:00:51.450
Engineers start with performance requirements,
tune deliberately, and keep the

15
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.362
controller as simple as the application
allows.

16
00:00:54.362 --> 00:00:58.818
A PI controller may be enough when derivative
action adds little value.
